  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 240 AM MDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nFlat upper level ridging is building across the forecast area as\nevident in the latest water vapor imagery, as well temporary \ndrying aloft. Meanwhile, a low level moisture surge of \nmoisture/cooling was originating from the convective complex in \nthe eastern portions of Kansas and Oklahoma. \n\nWith the ridging aloft, we'll be heating up again with plenty of \nsunshine. Summer-like heat is expected to return for the Front \nRange and I-25 Corridor, with highs in the metro areas reaching \nthe 90-95 degree range. There is more uncertainty about warming \nfarther east across the eastern plains, given the weak surge noted\nat the present time and also lack of lee troughing. A lot of the \ndetails hinge on the convective evolution in the Central Plains, \nbut we do see cooler temps (highs closer to 80F) over the far \nnortheast corner. With regard to convection, we do expect a late \nday/evening surge of mid/upper level moisture approaching from the\nDesert Southwest. Given we'll likely warm dry adiabatically to \n550 mb and DCAPE increases to nearly 1500 J/kg, the main threat \nfrom any showers and storms late this afternoon and evening would \nbe gusty microbursts. Peak winds could easily reach 50 mph given \nthe DCAPE. We'll continue with the low PoPs in the forecast (very \nlight rainfall) and added gusty winds as an attribute. \n\nMid and upper level monsoonal moisture will continue to increase\nlate tonight through Monday morning, which will keep a chance of\nshowers in the forecast. By Monday afternoon, the deepest plume\nshifts to areas southeast of I-76. At the same time, mid level\ncloud cover could also limit daytime heating (still very warm!), \nand keep a weak cap in place for the afternoon. Thus, the \ndevelopment of afternoon storms may be limited to the mountains \nand Palmer Divide area. We do get into the right entrance region \nof an upper level jet, so that could help carry some convection \neast/northeast across the I-25 Corridor and plains through Monday \nevening. \n\nA stronger cold front is still on track to arrive Monday night,\nushering in much cooler weather for Tuesday. Guidance continues to\nslowly slip for high temperatures, although we still think they \nmay be too warm. While deterministic forecasts still lie in the\nlower to mid 70s for the plains, forecast experience would \nsuggest many locations of the plains would more likely (>60% \nchance) stay in the upper 60s. Of course this will depend heavily\non extent of post-frontal stratus. That cooling also means we'll \nbe struggling to produce much instability, and any of that would \nhave to come from the mid levels and/or convective initiation over\nthe mountains. Overall the chances of precipitation have \ndecreased somewhat as the best plume of monsoonal moisture is now \nexpected to stay to our south.\n\nThat may change as we head closer to Wednesday but more likely\nThursday or even Friday. Guidance suggests a slower approach of\nthe next trough moving across the northern Great Basin and Central\nRockies. In fact, this leads to considerable uncertainty for the\nforecasts later this week, with Thursday now trending to be the \nwettest day (as hinted to yesterday). However, that trough is \nslowing so perhaps we could see daily chances of showers and \nstorms last into next weekend.",
